    <div class="faq-section">
      <h3 class="dept-services-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h3>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What facilities are available in the DHS Green ICU?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">Our Green ICU is equipped with invasive and non-invasive ventilators, continuous hemodynamic monitoring, HEPA-filtered isolation bays, central nursing monitoring, and 24/7 on-site critical care intensivists and specially trained ICU nursing staff.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What does "Green ICU" mean at DHS Hospital?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">Green ICU refers to our infection-controlled critical care design — HEPA-filtered positive-pressure air handling, dedicated isolation zones, controlled visitor movement, and a calmer, lower-noise environment — all aimed at reducing hospital-acquired infections and supporting faster patient recovery.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Who manages critical care patients at DHS Hospital?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">The ICU is led by Dr. Archit Parikh (MD Medicine — Infectious Disease, Critical Care &amp; ICU), supported by a multi-disciplinary team of specialists including orthopedic, oncology, and internal medicine consultants who coordinate care for complex, critically ill patients.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Are ICU visiting hours flexible for family members?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">ICU visiting is restricted to protect critically ill patients from infection and stress. A daily attendant briefing is provided by the treating team, and emergency updates are shared with family members as needed at any hour.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
